From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 03:52:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 344/353] soc: apple: s2r: optionally code T8132 commit
 read-backs in WD2's time

Four real T8132 sleeps on the J604 ended the same way: a counted reset
about 5 s after the commit point, the time WD2 is armed with before the
commit, with no wake reaching the kernel. An awake WD2 expiry leaves
the same record, so the SoC most likely never powered down: the sleep
engine did not take the commit. Nothing can report from the tail, as
there is no console and no SPMI there; only the time of the reset can
carry a reading.

Add two bring-up parameters to apple-s2r, both off by default.

t8132_commit_code selects one group of read-backs and arms WD2 again
with 8 s + 3 s per slot of the first finding (8 to 95 s, clamped below
WD1's commit time), so that a reset read with the RTC's one second
names it; nothing found keeps macOS's 5 s:

  1: an armed wake source whose status bit is already set, right before
     the commit (slot = index in the armed list)
  2: a quiesce power word still on (slot = index)
  3: the tail's own invariants, in order: DCS at its sleep state, the
     PMC voters at 0, the SoC at its sleep perf state, the MCC enables
     at 0, AUTO_ENABLE on the NUB SRAM word, this core's RVBAR locked to
     the verified vector, the CPU cluster words at 0
  4: what the commit register reads 3 ms after the commit (0, 1, other)

Groups 1 to 3 only read; group 4 re-arms WD2 after the commit, which
only matters when the engine has not taken it.

t8132_lower_stray: on a real sleep, once every coprocessor has stopped,
lower AVD_SYS, VENC_SYS, GFX and JPG if they are still on and wait for
each to go off, as Aurora does at its last step from macOS's sequence
(apple_sleep_lower_stray(), t8132_stray_words, in aurora-silicon/linux
PR 1314 by Bartosz Nitka and Diljit Singh, from the aura PR 109 trace).
One that will not go off is reported and reset with stage 0x3e, like
any refusal past the coprocessor stops. They are raised again after
the power words are woken on the return.

tools/asahi/t8132-commit-code-tests.py checks the time table and its
clamp, the groups, the read order, the defaults, the gating and the
raise order.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
